   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On 09/20/2015 12:09 AM, Phil Wood wrote:\r<br>&gt; I&#39;m somewhat puzzled by the following. Both Lavaan and Mplus converge,\r<br>&gt; but I&#39;m puzzled that the LL is different and that the paramter estimates\r<br>&gt; are so different. The example I show here is 300 observations, six\r<br>&gt; variables and no missing data.\r<br>&gt; I&#39;ve attached the lavaan program (factormeanshift.R), the Mplus program\r<br>&gt; (fmshift2.inp) and the data (sixtimegrowth.txt). Any ideas?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Blame the optimizer. Usually, the optimizer used by lavaan (nlminb) \r<br>performs really well. But every now and then, it may get stuck in a \r<br>local optimum (and think it converged). The flatter the parameter space, \r<br>the more likely this is to happen.\r<br>\r<br>In this particular example, Mplus and Onyx do find a better solution. In \r<br>other examples, lavaan finds the better solution. But in my experience, \r<br>discrepancies like these mostly happen for models that are very \r<br>mis-specified (note that the X^2 value is a factor 80-90 larger than the \r<br>degrees of freedom).\r<br>\r<br>It would appear that the following trick helps:\r<br>\r<br>fit &lt;- sem(FactorMeanShift.model, data = curvedata, \r<br>control=list(init_nelder_mead=TRUE), verbose = TRUE)\r<br>\r<br>(this forces a number of &#39;Nelder-Mead&#39; iterations before the \r<br>quasi-newton optimizer kicks in). Unfortunately, in almost all but a few \r<br>cases, this does not help at all.\r<br>\r<br>In lavaan 0.6, I will add support for external optimizers if they are \r<br>available (eg package nloptr), and if I can find an optimizer that \r<br>consistently performs better than nlminb(), I will make the switch.\r<br>\r<br>Yves.\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
